---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import PowerBIEmbed from '../../components/PowerBIEmbed.astro';
import CtnDashboard from '../../components/CtnDashboard.astro';
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="nursery-stock"
  facts={[
    ['Where', 'Trees for the Future, Kenya'],
    ['Tools', 'Power BI Desktop, Power Query, DAX, Python'],
    ['Source', 'One Excel log on SharePoint, updated weekly'],
  ]}
>  <PowerBIEmbed slug="nursery-stock" title="Nursery stock" />


  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The community tree nurseries report their stock in a shared Excel log. Each week, field officers enter how many
        seedlings each nursery holds, by species, growth stage and quality.
      </p>
      <p>
        A total taken from that workbook hides two things. It cannot say whether a number was counted this week, copied
        from last week or never entered. And because every week restates the same seedlings, adding weeks together
        counts them many times over.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I built a nine-page Power BI dashboard that reads the log straight from SharePoint. The model has 10 tables, 11
        relationships and 58 measures. A Python script generates the whole project, and a second script checks it
        before it is opened in Power BI.
      </p>
      <p>The rules it applies are written on the last page of the dashboard, so readers can check them:</p>
      <ul class="list">
        <li>
          Stock is each nursery's own latest report on or before the week shown, added across nurseries. Weeks are
          never added together.
        </li>
        <li>
          A report that matches the one before it, line for line, is marked as copied. Two in a row is flagged as not
          recounted, and a missed week as not updated.
        </li>
        <li>
          A fall of 20 percent or more, and of 500 seedlings or more, with no comment saying stock was distributed or
          disposed of, is listed as an unexplained drop.
        </li>
        <li>
          The sprout rate is grafts sprouting or ready over all grafts. It is shown only while some grafts are still
          healing, because after that there is nothing left to compare.
        </li>
        <li>
          Entries dated in a future year are matched to the officers' entry log and moved to the right year. Each
          repair is still listed, because the workbook itself holds the typo.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One page lists every row that needs fixing, with the workbook row to open, so it can be sent back to the
        officer who entered it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtnDashboard />

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Reading the demo</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This is a web rebuild of four of the nine pages on a generated log. Pick an earlier week to see the dashboard as
        it stood then. On the weekly reporting page, compare Nursery A with Nursery B: both file most weeks, but B often
        files the same numbers again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The stock over time page shows why weeks are not added together. The columns are what was entered each week
        and dip whenever a nursery skips a week. The line carries each nursery's latest report forward and does not.
      </p>
      <p>The generated log has no comments, so every large fall in it counts as unexplained.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Result</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        On its first run against the real log, the dashboard showed nurseries filing identical reports several weeks in
        a row, a week in which no nursery reported, and a large fall in recorded stock with no distribution or disposal
        comment against it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The dashboard reads the 2026 log only, and a 2027 log means changing one file name in the queries. It has no
        phone layout yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    list-style: square;
  }

  .list ::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
